  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Locally dense fog and probably widespread low \novercast this morning, then showers and isolated storms focused over \nthe Piedmont and Sandhills this afternoon\n\nCyclonic, nnwly flow aloft will prevail across the Middle and South \nAtlantic states through tonight, between a slow-moving cyclone over \nthe Canadian Maritimes and a ridge over the srn High Plains. Within \nthat nnwly flow regime, at least a couple of impulses over the mid \nand upr MS Valley will progress across the Southeast.\n\nAt the surface, a synoptic front was analyzed at 06Z from far nern \nNC nwwd across srn VA, then generally wwd through KY and srn MO. \nThis boundary will continue to settle swwd, in backdoor fashion, \nthough NC today. \n\nLight wind, initially clear skies, and abundant near surface and \nsoil moisture will favor the development of areas of low stratus and \nfog/mist this morning. While related ceilings and visibility \nrestrictions will lift and disperse through midday, an area of \ndeeper, 850-700 mb centered moisture stretching across the Virginias \nand lwr Oh Valley will progress ssewd into cntl NC through the \nafternoon. This area of moisture will coincide with the progression \nof the backdoor surface cold front and result in the development and \ndeepening of showers and isolated storms, focused over the Piedmont \nand adjacent Sandhills, where diurnal timing will allow for \nrelatively stronger heating and destabilization. An isolated strong \nto severe storm or two will be possible over the far wrn/srn \nPiedmont during the afternoon, before the front and moisture and \ninstability axes continue wswwd into the Upstate of SC and far swrn \nNC tonight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Several days of near normal temperatures starting \ntoday, followed by an increase back above normal mid/late week \n\nTemperatures today through Tuesday should remain near normal as a \nmid/upper level longwave trough slowly shifts off the east coast. \nCooler and drier air should filter into the region from the north \nthrough the day today, allowing high temperatures this afternoon to \nrise into the low 80s north to the upper 80s south. Temperatures \nshould remain in the 80s through at least Tuesday. As ridging starts \nto build over the region mid-week, highs should return to the 90s on \nWednesday or Thursday. The chance of rain early this week after \ntoday will be generally low each day, with an isolated diurnal \nthunderstorm possible each afternoon/evening especially late week as \nwarming is occurring and moisture is returning to the region. It \nlooks like another cold front may pass through central NC Thursday \nnight which brings the next best chance of showers to the region. \nTemperatures may then cool off a few degrees, potentially back into \nthe 80s, for next weekend.",
